UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 36, Lesson 1: Grid references and maps', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Grid references locate cells or points on maps using letters and numbers (or coordinates) in an agreed order.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nOn a grid, what is one cell right of C2 if columns increase A→D to the right?\n\nFEEDBACK\nD2.\n\nWATCH FOR\nSwapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nLocate twenty grid references and give references for twelve marked cells. State the order rule you used.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Grid references locate cells or points on maps using letters and numbers (or coordinates) in an agreed order.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Concept","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-GM-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Locate twenty grid references and give references for twelve marked cells. State the order rule you used. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for grid; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Swapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place.. Correction uses D2.. Why: restores accurate grid.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Swapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses grid with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with grid, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for grid, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about grid, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for grid. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","D2","Swapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"On a grid, what is one cell right of C2 if columns increase A→D to the right?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for grid; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Grid references locate cells or points on maps using letters and numbers (or coordinates) in an agreed order","D2","Careful use of reference and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Swapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with grid?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named reference and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply grid to new material and name the reference and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Swapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place","Name reference and explain why it matters only, without linking it to grid"],"question":"Which response best applies grid with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Swapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place","Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid","Write only a final number with no working","Change digits to look neater without checking place value"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on grid?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught grid method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Read 2 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value","Ignore units and treat 2 and 10 as interchangeable","D2","Swapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place"],"question":"Using the method for grid, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically","Delete units so the answers appear to match","Average the two answers without checking either"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a grid problem with numbers such as 2 and 10, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers","Swapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place","Use the same grid method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter","Retype the worked model numbers 2 and 10 as the answer"],"question":"Solve a fresh grid problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["D2","Label units when comparing 2 with 10","Swapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place","Grid references locate cells or points on maps using letters and numbers (or coordinates) in an agreed order"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for grid?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for grid, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses grid with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for grid; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about grid, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with grid, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Swapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place.. Correction uses D2.. Why: restores accurate grid.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y4-w36-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise grid.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Measurement and Space","success_criteria":["I can use grid with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify reference and explain why it matters.","I can practise map independently on a new prompt.","I can notice cell needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Grid references locate cells or points on maps using letters and numbers (or coordinates) in an agreed order. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Grid references locate cells or points on maps using letters and numbers (or coordinates) in an agreed order","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about grid with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCell B3 means column B, row 3 (confirm the key). Moving right increases the letter/column; moving up increases the row number on many maps.\n\nTRY FIRST\nOn a grid, what is one cell right of C2 if columns increase A→D to the right?\n\nFEEDBACK\nD2.","explain":"Grid references locate cells or points on maps using letters and numbers (or coordinates) in an agreed order.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Swapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Grid references on maps.","caption":"Do not swap order.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Grid ref</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">letter + number</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Order</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">agreed key</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Cell B3 means column B, row 3 (confirm the key). Moving right increases the letter/column; moving up increases the row n Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Cell B3 means column B, row 3 (confirm the key). Moving right increases the letter/column; moving up increases the row n Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about grid with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCell B3 means column B, row 3 (confirm the key). Moving right increases the letter/column; moving up increases the row number on many maps. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Correct cell vs swapped ref.","caption":"Twenty locates.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">C2 → D2</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">one right</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">swap letter/number</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about grid with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSwapping letter and number order sends you to the wrong place. Here is a more productive model: Cell B3 means column B, row 3 (confirm the key). Moving right increases the letter/column; moving up increases the row number on many maps.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\nD2.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\n